{#
The dialog behind every `data-turbo-confirm` in the application.

Turbo's confirmation calls `window.confirm()` unless something says otherwise,
and the browser dialog is the one piece of UI the app cannot style, cannot
translate consistently, cannot theme, and which announces the origin at the top
as though the page were doing something suspicious. Twenty-two places used it —
deleting a label, running a filter over existing mail, resetting the database —
and each looked like a security prompt rather than part of plMail.

One dialog for all of them, and it is `Turbo.setConfirmMethod` that connects the
two (assets/confirm.js), so this is not twenty-two changes: any
`data-turbo-confirm` written after today gets it for free, which is the only
version of this fix that stays fixed.

`<dialog>` rather than a div. showModal() puts the element in the browser's top
layer, which is above every stacking context on the page — so this cannot be
trapped behind a pane the way the app's other popovers can be (the panes use
backdrop-filter, which makes them a containing block for fixed positioning too).
It also brings focus trapping, restoring focus to the trigger on close, the
Escape key, and inertness of the rest of the page, all of which would otherwise
be hand-written.

It looks like the app's modal (_modal.html.twig) because it is the same kind of
object: one continuous surface, spacing rather than rules to separate the title
from the question, actions bottom-right, and the two footer buttons copied from
_modal_form.html.twig rather than re-invented a fifth time.

What it CANNOT borrow from the modal is the box itself. The modal draws its own
backdrop element and centres its panel with flexbox inside it; a <dialog> has
neither — the browser paints ::backdrop, and the centring is the UA's
`margin: auto`, which Tailwind's preflight zeroes along with every other margin
in the document. That is why the geometry and the dim live in app.css under
`#confirm-dialog` and not in the class list here: see the block there.
#}
<dialog id="confirm-dialog"
        data-controller="ui--confirm"
        aria-labelledby="confirm-dialog-title"
        {# Same entrance as the modal panel, for the same reason: this is the
           largest thing that ever appears over the page, and a whole screen
           changing state in one frame is the jump cut motion exists to answer.
           Safe to declare on markup that is in the document from first paint,
           because a closed <dialog> is display:none and a display:none element
           has no running animation — the entrance starts at showModal() and
           again on every open after that. Reduced motion is handled where it
           is handled for everything else, by motion.css zeroing the tokens. #}
        data-enter="pop"
        class="pane-flat shadow-float text-ink">
    <form method="dialog" class="flex flex-col gap-4 p-6">
        <h2 id="confirm-dialog-title" class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">
            {{ 'confirm_dialog.heading'|trans }}
        </h2>

        {# `whitespace-pre-line` because the question is plain text written into
           a `data-turbo-confirm` attribute, and several of them are two
           sentences with a line break between them. #}
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft whitespace-pre-line"
           data-ui--confirm-target="message"></p>

        <div class="flex items-center justify-end gap-2 pt-1">
            {# `value` is what <form method="dialog"> hands back as returnValue,
               which is how the controller learns which button was pressed
               without a listener on either. #}
            <button type="submit" value="cancel"
                    data-ui--confirm-target="cancel"
                    class="inline-flex items-center justify-center h-8 px-3.5 rounded-lg text-sm font-medium
                           text-ink-soft
                           bg-field
                           border border-field
                           hover:bg-hover
                           focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-accent focus-visible:ring-offset-1
                           transition-colors duration-100">
                {{ 'confirm_dialog.cancel'|trans }}
            </button>

            {# Accent rather than red, even though every caller is destructive.
               The red belongs to the control that OPENED this — the delete
               button on the row — and repeating it here would leave the two
               weightiest colours on screen sitting beside a quiet Cancel, which
               is how a confirmation stops being read at all. The safety is that
               Cancel holds the focus, not that Continue is frightening. #}
            <button type="submit" value="confirm"
                    data-ui--confirm-target="confirm"
                    class="inline-flex items-center justify-center h-8 px-3.5 rounded-lg text-sm font-medium
                           text-accent-ink
                           bg-accent hover:bg-accent-strong active:bg-accent-strong
                           focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-accent focus-visible:ring-offset-1
                           transition-colors duration-100">
                {{ 'confirm_dialog.confirm'|trans }}
            </button>
        </div>
    </form>
</dialog>
